    recovery: "Cough, sore throat and transient wheeze can follow removal. The team checks for retained fragments and treats infection only when indicated; recurrent aspiration may prompt swallowing or neurologic assessment. Travel waits until airway, oxygen and anaesthesia recovery are stable.",
    risks: "Risks include pushing or dropping the object into another airway, mucosal tear, bleeding, swelling, bronchospasm, pneumothorax, perforation, infection, dental or laryngeal injury and anaesthetic complications. Complete obstruction during extraction can cause severe hypoxia and requires immediate rigid-airway rescue.",
