    procedure: "Chest Tube / Intercostal Drainage", shortName: "chest-tube drainage", cluster: "Pleural disease", specialist: "pulmonologist, emergency physician or thoracic surgeon trained in pleural drainage", unit: "per drain episode",
    definition: "A chest tube or intercostal drain passes through the chest wall into the pleural space to evacuate air, fluid, blood, pus or chyle. It connects to an underwater seal or other one-way system so the lung can re-expand while output and air leak are monitored.",
    mechanism: "Ultrasound identifies fluid and diaphragm; pneumothorax uses clinical and imaging landmarks. A small-bore catheter may enter over a guidewire by Seldinger technique, while blunt dissection places a larger tube with finger confirmation where appropriate. Side holes remain inside the pleura and the external system stays below chest level; suction is added only for a defined indication.",
